# docker-compose.yml reads this file for the broker container.
BIND_HOST=0.0.0.0
PORT=8788
RUST_LOG=relay_broker=info,tower_http=info
RELAY_BROKER_AUTH_MODE=self_hosted
RELAY_BROKER_TICKET_SECRET=change-me
# Optional: set a finite lifetime for saved device broker join credentials.
# RELAY_BROKER_DEVICE_JOIN_TTL_SECS=2592000
# Public broker mode instead uses broker-issued short-lived ws tokens plus
# refresh credentials. Example broker env:
# RELAY_BROKER_AUTH_MODE=public
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLIC_ISSUER_SECRET=change-me
# Required when BIND_HOST is non-loopback in public mode. Use EITHER the JSON
# state path OR a Postgres URL (mutually exclusive). Postgres is recommended for
# durability and is required before multi-broker/HA.
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLIC_STATE_PATH=/var/lib/agent-relay/public-control.json
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLIC_POSTGRES_URL=postgres://user:pass@host/db
# Cross-instance revocation visibility (Postgres only; NOT full HA). By default a
# single broker keeps the in-memory control plane authoritative and SKIPS
# reloading from Postgres before every op (much lower latency). Set this to 1 if
# more than one broker process can run against the same database at once —
# including brief blue/green or rolling-deploy overlap — so each instance re-reads
# committed state before every op; otherwise a revoke/rotation on one instance is
# not observed by another until it restarts. This only bounds how stale a read can
# be; it does NOT serialize invariants — e.g. two instances can each pass the same
# device-limit check and both insert a grant. True multi-broker HA needs
# database-level locking.
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLIC_POSTGRES_RELOAD_BEFORE_USE=1
# Rotation grace window (seconds; default 172800 = 48h). Every approval rotates
# the client/device refresh tokens, but the fresh token only reaches the session
# that completes that pairing handshake — an already-paired device that never
# sees it (duplicate approve, stale request, lost delivery) keeps authenticating
# with its previous token for this window, sliding forward while in active use.
# Explicit revocation still cuts off current AND superseded tokens immediately.
# Set to 0 to disable the grace window (strict rotation).
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLIC_ROTATION_GRACE_SECS=172800
# Optional: usage event stream (connect/disconnect/publish analytics). Postgres
# takes precedence over the file path when both are set; point it at the same DB
# as RELAY_BROKER_PUBLIC_POSTGRES_URL to keep analytics in one place.
# RELAY_BROKER_USAGE_EVENTS_POSTGRES_URL=postgres://user:pass@host/db
# RELAY_BROKER_USAGE_EVENTS_PATH=/var/lib/agent-relay/usage-events.ndjson
# Optional: cap usage_events table growth. A background task deletes rows older
# than this many days (unset / 0 = keep forever; Postgres sink only).
# RELAY_BROKER_USAGE_EVENTS_RETENTION_DAYS=90
# Optional: IP blocklist. Point at the same DB; reject banned IPs/CIDRs with 403.
# Ban via: INSERT INTO banned_ips (ip_or_cidr, reason) VALUES ('1.2.3.4','abuse');
# RELAY_BROKER_BANNED_IPS_POSTGRES_URL=postgres://user:pass@host/db
# Behind a reverse proxy the socket IP is the proxy's, so IP bans (and per-IP
# rate limits) would target the proxy. Set this to the header the proxy adds with
# the real client IP so bans apply to the actual client. Leave unset for direct
# connections / local dev (uses the socket IP; forwarded headers are NOT trusted).
# SECURITY: only set this if the broker is reachable ONLY through the trusted
# proxy (bind private / not publicly routable). If clients can reach the broker
# directly, they can forge this header to spoof or evade bans and rate limits.
# RELAY_BROKER_TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER=cf-connecting-ip
# Optional public broker hardening:
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLIC_API_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=120
# RELAY_BROKER_JOIN_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=40
# Publish allowance for a surface peer (a browser tab sending user-driven actions).
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLISH_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=240
# Publish allowance for a relay peer, which is first-party and an order of magnitude
# busier: transcript deltas alone batch into a 100ms window and publish one frame each.
# Going over makes the broker DROP that frame, which the relay treats as fatal — it ends
# the session and reconnects to resync rather than carry on with a hole in what the
# surface received. So setting this below real traffic does not merely slow things down,
# it makes the relay reconnect repeatedly, and the broker's window is keyed by peer so
# it does not reset on reconnect. This bounds frames, not bytes.
# If you set the generic limit above and leave this unset, the generic one governs
# relays too, so an existing tightened deployment is not silently widened on upgrade.
# RELAY_BROKER_RELAY_PUBLISH_RATE_LIMIT_PER_MINUTE=36000
# Byte allowance, the bandwidth counterpart to the frame allowances above. A frame count
# says nothing about size: 36000 frames at the 64KiB cap is ~2.2GiB a minute from one
# peer. These bound that. Same fatal-on-refusal caveat as the frame limits — set the relay
# one below real traffic and the relay reconnects in a loop, and each reconnect resyncs a
# full snapshot, so it costs MORE bandwidth than it saves. The relay default is ~6.8x the
# chunked-reply ceiling of 75MiB/min. Do NOT re-derive that from the 32KiB chunk target:
# that is a PAYLOAD size and the frame carrying it is bigger. The 75MiB/min figure is
# instead the EFFECTIVE frame cap (RELAY_BROKER_MAX_TEXT_FRAME_BYTES, which has a 64KiB
# floor — see below) times the 50ms publish cadence, which the relay's chunk builders do
# respect because they fit against that same 64KiB. Set either to 0 to disable the byte
# budget entirely.
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLISH_BYTES_PER_MINUTE=8388608
# RELAY_BROKER_RELAY_PUBLISH_BYTES_PER_MINUTE=536870912
# Burst is a separate quota from the rate: how much a peer may publish back-to-back after
# idling. Without it the first large reply after a quiet period is refused, which is
# exactly when one is most likely (a tab opens and asks for a workspace diff). The relay
# default swallows a ~4MiB diff whole. A burst below RELAY_BROKER_MAX_TEXT_FRAME_BYTES is
# raised to it, since a smaller one could never afford a single legal frame.
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLISH_BURST_BYTES=2097152
# RELAY_BROKER_RELAY_PUBLISH_BURST_BYTES=16777216
# RELAY_BROKER_MAX_CONNECTIONS_PER_IP=24
# Has a 64KiB FLOOR: relay-server fits chunks against a fixed 64KiB limit compiled into its
# binary and cannot learn this value, so a smaller cap rejects frames rather than shrinking
# them — and the broker closes the socket on frame_too_large, so the replay fails the same
# way. Values below 65536 are raised with a warning. Larger values work normally.
# RELAY_BROKER_MAX_TEXT_FRAME_BYTES=65536
# RELAY_BROKER_IDLE_TIMEOUT_SECS=120
# Optional: tighten production broker page CSP connect-src. Leave unset for
# dev/LAN-friendly defaults.
# RELAY_BROKER_CSP_CONNECT_SRC='self' https://broker.example.com wss://broker.example.com
# Enable only behind HTTPS with X-Forwarded-Proto: https
# RELAY_BROKER_ENABLE_HSTS=1
# Optional: override the default HSTS policy.
# RELAY_BROKER_HSTS_VALUE=max-age=86400
# Optional operator stats endpoint GET /api/admin/stats (per-relay device/client
# counts, busiest first; Bearer this token). Unset = endpoint disabled (404).
# Keep it independent of user creds and reachable only behind your proxy / on a
# trusted network.
# RELAY_BROKER_ADMIN_TOKEN=change-me-long-random

# relay-server should usually run outside this compose stack, close to the repo
# and Codex CLI session. Example:
#
# BIND_HOST=127.0.0.1 \
# PORT=8787 \
# RELAY_API_TOKEN=change-me \
# RELAY_SECURITY_MODE=private \
# RELAY_BROKER_URL=ws://127.0.0.1:8788 \
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLIC_URL=ws://192.168.1.105:8788 \
# RELAY_BROKER_AUTH_MODE=self_hosted \
# RELAY_BROKER_CHANNEL_ID=dev-room \
# RELAY_BROKER_PEER_ID=local-relay \
# RELAY_BROKER_TICKET_SECRET=change-me \
# RELAY_BROKER_DEVICE_JOIN_TTL_SECS=2592000 \
# cargo run -p relay-server
#
# When BIND_HOST is non-loopback, RELAY_API_TOKEN is required by default.
# RELAY_ALLOW_INSECURE_NO_AUTH=1 only exists as an explicit insecure
# development override.
# Optional: tighten production relay page CSP connect-src. Leave unset for
# dev/LAN-friendly defaults.
# RELAY_CSP_CONNECT_SRC='self' https://relay.example.com wss://broker.example.com
# Enable only behind HTTPS with X-Forwarded-Proto: https
# RELAY_ENABLE_HSTS=1
# Optional: override the default HSTS policy.
# RELAY_HSTS_VALUE=max-age=86400
#
# Public mode relay example:
#
# RELAY_BROKER_URL=wss://broker.example.com \
# RELAY_BROKER_PUBLIC_URL=wss://broker.example.com \
# RELAY_BROKER_CONTROL_URL=https://broker.example.com \
# RELAY_BROKER_AUTH_MODE=public \
# RELAY_BROKER_PEER_ID=local-relay \
# cargo run -p relay-server
#
# RELAY_BROKER_REGISTRATION_PATH / RELAY_BROKER_IDENTITY_PATH default to the
# relay's state directory (~/.agent-relay/, or wherever RELAY_STATE_PATH points),
# next to session.json. Overriding one to a location OUTSIDE that directory
# splits the relay's identity set: it can then fail to find its registration,
# enroll as a brand new relay, and orphan already-paired devices. The relay
# warns at startup when that happens. See DEPLOYMENT.md#relay-state-location.
#
# On first startup without a cached registration, the relay automatically
# creates a local signing key, completes the broker enrollment challenge, and
# caches relay_id + broker_room_id + relay_refresh_token locally.
